" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TPR=\frac{tp}{tp+fn}\\
</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct repeated experiments with random train and test splits using different seeds to estimate the expected performance of the models on the detection task. The seeds are used to
both initialize machine learning model parameters and create varying splits of the data.
We then report the mean and standard deviation of selected metrics across 30 independent model training and testing runs.</p>
</div>
